          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 5-year-old child presents with bruises of varying colours on the back and ears. The parent reports the child is 'always falling while playing.' Which feature of this presentation most reliably points toward non-accidental injury?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The child's young age of 5 years</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Age alone does not distinguish accidental from non-accidental injury. School-age children sustain accidental injuries frequently.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bruises of different ages in locations uncommon for accidental falls</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Bruises at different stages of healing indicate repeated injury, and locations such as the back and ears are rarely injured in typical childhood falls — shins, foreheads, and elbows are the usual accidental sites.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The parent bringing the child to a hospital</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Seeking medical care does not by itself suggest abuse. Many abusive caregivers do present children for treatment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The child presenting during evening hours</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The timing of presentation has no established correlation with the mechanism of injury.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The key is pattern recognition: bruises of different ages suggest repeated injury, and locations like the back and ears are uncommon for accidental childhood falls. Together with a history–injury mismatch, these form the triad for recognising non-accidental injury."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Different-aged bruises in unusual locations (back, ears, buttocks) combined with a history–injury mismatch form the recognition triad for non-accidental injury."
                }
              ],
