          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 4-year-old is brought to the emergency department in anaphylactic shock after </p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 4-year-old is brought to the emergency department in anaphylactic shock after an insect sting. The child's single parent is unreachable by phone and the accompanying neighbour has no legal authority to consent. What is the physician's correct course of action?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Administer adrenaline immediately under the doctrine of necessity</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. When the authorised person cannot be reached and delay would cause serious harm or death, the doctrine of necessity permits the physician to intervene in the child's best interest without formal consent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Wait until the parent can be contacted to obtain informed consent</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Waiting would be dangerous in anaphylaxis. The doctrine of necessity specifically addresses this scenario — the child's right to life-saving treatment overrides the requirement for formal parental consent.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Obtain written consent from the neighbour and proceed with treatment</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A neighbour without legal guardianship cannot provide valid consent for a minor. Proceeding on invalid consent does not offer legal protection.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Refer the child to a higher centre where a legal team can advise</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Referral with a delay in anaphylaxis management could be fatal. The physician has both an ethical and legal duty to act under the doctrine of necessity.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In a life-threatening emergency where the legal guardian is unreachable, the doctrine of necessity permits the physician to treat the child without formal consent. The child's best interest — survival — takes precedence."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "The doctrine of necessity allows physicians to intervene without consent when delay would cause serious harm and the authorised person is unavailable — a vital safeguard for paediatric emergencies."
                }
              ],
